[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The same type of VFX that used to take a film studio weeks now takes me about 5 minutes in one browser tab and it works on footage you already shot. So your face and performance stay exactly the same while everything around [music] you gets changed. In this video, I will walk'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you through the entire process on how you can do that and make your content look professional. So every effect I\'m about to show you runs on one base video and I\'m not even going to pick up a camera to make it. Normally, this is'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where you\'d go shoot something or use an AI video tool that generates a random from scratch and gives you something very generic. So instead, I head straight into OpenArt which is the tool'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m using for this whole build. And if you want to do the same, the link to sign up is right down in the description. Once I\'m inside OpenArt, I go to video. Then I switch it over to text with reference and grab SeaDance'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2.0. The reference part is important because I just upload a photo of my face, tag it as image one and now everything SeaDance makes actually looks like me. I keep the settings as simple as possible. 8 seconds for duration,'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '1080p for quality, 16 by 9 aspect ratio, the audio on and then I just describe what I want. Which is me sitting in a plain lived-in living room with a mug of coffee, taking a sip and glancing'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around. I hit generate and here\'s what I get. It\'s me in a usual room and doing nothing special and it\'s exactly what I wanted. So now I\'ve got my starting video. The only catch is that boring room behind me and getting rid of it'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'without a green screen or a single reshoot is the first real trick. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; So the room is the first thing to go and the way OpenArt handles it is honestly why I stopped bothering with green screens. First, I build the room that I'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to be in. I head to image, choose GPT image two and keep the settings simple. 16 by 9 for the aspect ratio, 1K for the resolution, quality on high. Then I just describe what I want. A'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bright cozy cafe with big windows, warm wood floors, and a few plants, [music] and some people in the background, too. I will explain in a minute why I added that. Then I generate it and get a nice wide shot of the cafe and the people'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inside it. Now for the swap, I go to video, open replace background, drop in my original video, add that cafe image as the reference, and generate. And the reason the result feels believable is'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that OpenArt is not treating this like a basic cutout job, where it removes the old background and simply drops a new one behind you. It is rebuilding the shot in a way that matches the perspective, depth, and lighting of the'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'original footage. So, I still feel properly anchored in the frame instead of looking separated from it. The dull room is now gone, and I\'m sitting in a cafe that was never there. And this is where most people would stop because'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'technically the background has already been replaced. But, if you want the shot to feel more controlled and more cinematic, the next step isn\'t changing the background again. It\'s changing the way the scene is lit. And no, the fix'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'isn\'t dragging a warm filter over the top because that only tints the image without actually rebuilding the lighting. That\'s exactly why the next tool matters. The tool is called relight in OpenArt, and what it actually does is'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rebuild the light source across the entire frame. [music] So, instead of just shifting my color, it lights the cafe and me from the same source at the same angle. That matters because when the light on you and the light behind you mismatch, [music]'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your brain thinks the shot is a fake one even if you can\'t say why. So, I take my cafe video into the video section in OpenArt. [music] Then I open relight and run it twice to show you how far it goes. The first'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] pass uses the golden hour glow preset. As you can see compared to what we had previously, the golden light has naturally fallen over me as it would if I were to sit beside a window. Then I switch the preset to moonlit [music]'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'blue and run it again. Same 8 seconds, but now the whole scene shifts from warm golden light to a cooler moonlit blue look, and the lighting on me changes to match [music] the new preset on the side. Two completely different styles,'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and all I changed was one drop down. I\'m keeping the golden one going forward since it fits the cafe best. And those two presets are just two of the ones you\'ve got to pick from. If I had a'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really specific look in mind, let\'s say a cozy warm vlog, tense thriller, or any brand\'s exact color palette, I don\'t have to settle for one preset at all. I can feed Relight a reference image instead, and it builds the entire mood'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the shot off that one picture, matching the color, the atmosphere, and the way the light behaves to whatever I gave it. Now that the lighting matches the room, I finally look like I\'m actually sitting in [music] that cafe.'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The shot is perfect now, except for those people behind me, and this is where I tell you why I decided to keep them. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; The reason is that the last tool can erase anything in the shot except me. So, I wanted something back there that'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was actually worth erasing. This is the mode Open Art named the whole feature after, VFX. What it does is let me change one specific [music] part of the shot, while everything else, my face, my movement, even my audio stays exactly as'

[05:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I filmed. So, I open VFX in video, click select mask, and paint a mask over everything except the people [music] in the background. Then I tell it what to do with that masked area, which is to'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'remove those people, but keep the desk [music] and the chair. I hit generate, and compared to the video from a second ago, the whole crowd behind me is simply gone. This is what makes VFX useful for small controlled edits. You can remove'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'people, clean up distractions, replace objects, or adjust one detail without rebuilding the whole video. So, if one clip is almost right, but one thing ruins it, you just mask that exact area'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and fix it. And because it all happens inside the same Open Art workflow, you can keep refining the shot without jumping between different tools. As you can see, this is the video I began with, and this is the video now. Same 8'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'seconds, same me, but it genuinely looks like a very different and premium production video. So, that\'s the ceiling of what it can do, but how good your own result comes out still starts with the actual video you use. None of this needs'

[06:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a fancy camera. Your phone is completely [music] fine, but a few small things at the filming stage give Open Art a lot more to work with. The biggest one is making sure you actually stand out from whatever is behind you, because the'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clearer the separation between you and your background, the better [music] it can separate you out and rebuild the other things. And filming against a busy and cluttered wall you blend into is the fastest way to get distorted character'

[06:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'edges. Next, light yourself softly and evenly, [music] and avoid harsh shadows or overly bright highlights, because once part of the frame is just pure black or pure white, there\'s no detail left for the [music] AI to relight. It'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also helps to lock your exposure and white balance, so the camera doesn\'t slowly adjust them halfway through the video and mess with the lighting you\'re trying to [music] rebuild. And keep the camera as stable as you can. A cheap'

[06:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tripod or even a properly held phone is better than getting shaky [music] footage every time, because steady framing is what lets the effect hold across the whole video instead of letting it glitch. Also, think about'

[07:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what you\'re wearing. [music] Avoid colors that blend into the background. So, if you\'re against a white wall, don\'t wear plain [music] white, and if the room is dark, don\'t wear all black. That bit of contrast helps Open Art read your outline'

[07:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'properly, which means cleaner edges around your arms, shoulders, and hair. Do those few things, and you\'re basically [music] giving Open Art the best foundation to work from, and the output comes back looking like you spent'

[07:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'far more on it than [music] you actually did. And honestly, I\'m not even close to done with that little coffee shop video. On the annual Wonder plan, each generation [music] only runs about 40 cents in Openart. So, I can keep'

[07:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'changing it into new looks for a couple of dollars total. So, sign up to Openart using my link in the description [music] and run your own video through VFX today. Thanks for watching and I\'ll see you in the next one.'
